\description{
Estimate Improvement in R-Squared
and Generate the Corresponding Sampling Distribution
Using Nonparametric Bootstrapping
}
\details{
The vector of improvement in R-squared
(\eqn{\Delta R^{2}})
is estimated from bootstrap samples.
Confidence intervals are generated by obtaining
percentiles corresponding to \eqn{100(1 - \alpha)\%}
from the generated sampling
distribution of \eqn{\Delta R^{2}},
where \eqn{\alpha} is the significance level.

With \code{adj = TRUE}, estimates and draws are differences between the
adjusted R-squared values of the full model and the model without
each regressor. The population target remains the unadjusted increment.
Negative adjusted estimates and draws are retained.

With \code{type = "mpc"} in \code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} or \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}, an unadjusted
lower limit is set to zero when the sample F test is nonsignificant.
For adjusted estimates, a nonpositive percentile lower limit becomes
\code{.001} if significant, or zero if nonsignificant; positive lower
limits are retained. Upper limits are unchanged, without clipping.
Both modes use the unadjusted sample F statistic and test at the
interval's error probability \code{alpha} (equality is nonsignificant).

These lower-limit rules follow Algina et al. (2007, p. 210;
2008a, pp. 3--5; 2008b, pp. 735, 737; 2010, pp. 928--929, 931--932).
Unlike their ranked endpoints, betaNB retains its ordinary
interpolated percentile calculation. Their studies used pairs
resampling; coverage for wild draws or heteroskedastic F testing
is not established by those studies.

Let \eqn{\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\ast}} be the vector of standardized slopes,
\eqn{\boldsymbol{R}_{x}} the correlation matrix of the regressors,
\eqn{r^{jj}} the \eqn{j}th diagonal element of \eqn{\boldsymbol{R}_{x}^{-1}},
and \eqn{R^{2} = \boldsymbol{\beta}^{\ast\prime} \boldsymbol{R}_{x}
\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\ast}}.
The semipartial correlation of regressor \eqn{j} is
\deqn{sr_{j} = \beta^{\ast}_{j} / \sqrt{r^{jj}} = \beta^{\ast}_{j} \sqrt{1 -
R^{2}_{j \cdot -j}},}
where \eqn{R^{2}_{j \cdot -j}} is the R-squared from regressing regressor
\eqn{j}
on the other regressors.
Its square, \eqn{sr^{2}_{j}}, is the change in \eqn{R^{2}} when regressor
\eqn{j} is added last.
Because \eqn{1 / r^{jj} = 1 - R^{2}_{j \cdot -j}}, \eqn{sr_{j}} shrinks
toward zero
as regressor \eqn{j} becomes more redundant with the other regressors.
The improvement in R-squared for regressor \eqn{j} is
\deqn{\Delta R^{2}_{j} = R^{2} - R^{2}_{-j} = sr^{2}_{j}.}
Each effect size is computed from every bootstrap sample
(see \code{\link[=NB]{NB()}}), and the confidence intervals are percentile-based.
}
\examples{
# Data ---------------------------------------------------------------------
data("nas1982", package = "betaNB")

# Fit Model in lm ----------------------------------------------------------
object <- stats::lm(
  formula = QUALITY ~ NARTIC + PCTGRT + PCTSUPP,
  data = nas1982
)

# NB -----------------------------------------------------------------------
nb <- NB(
  object = object,
  R = 100, # use a large value e.g., 5000L for actual research
  seed = 0508
)

# DeltaRSqNB ---------------------------------------------------------------
out <- DeltaRSqNB(object = nb, alpha = 0.05)

## Methods -----------------------------------------------------------------
print(x = out)
summary(object = out)
coef(object = out)
vcov(object = out)
confint(object = out, level = 0.95)
confint(object = out, level = 0.95, type = "mpc")
adjusted <- DeltaRSqNB(object = nb, alpha = 0.05, adj = TRUE)
confint(object = adjusted, level = 0.95, type = "mpc")

}
